WEBVTT

00:00:05.973 --> 00:00:09.012
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> Is family always more important than friends?

00:00:11.206 --> 00:00:14.864
<v><b>Alice:</b></v> I think, for the majority of people, that is the case,

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:20.764
but I also think we can’t deny the fact that families come in all shapes and sizes.

00:00:20.828 --> 00:00:25.775
We’re not just that, kind of, parents, children, er, format any more, because,

00:00:26.594 --> 00:00:30.774
you can’t deny that, maybe, children have not had, like, the best upbringing,

00:00:30.775 --> 00:00:34.930
and they feel like they’ve, they’ve had to, you know, separate from their parents,

00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:38.772
or vice versa because of external things.

00:00:38.812 --> 00:00:45.109
Um, so, they form these attachments with their friends that become like a family to them

00:00:45.182 --> 00:00:46.822
and become incredibly important.

00:00:46.928 --> 00:00:47.581
<v><b>James:</b></v> Yeah.

00:00:47.868 --> 00:00:54.436
<v><b>Alice:</b></v> Um, there are people that I know now, who, they are, um, what’s the word?

00:00:54.503 --> 00:01:01.671
Um, they’ve broken off from their, their parents because of things that have happened to them,

00:01:01.978 --> 00:01:07.888
um, but they have formed these attachments to their friends that are thicker than blood, almost.

00:01:07.921 --> 00:01:13.200
<v><b>James:</b></v> Yeah. That’s really interesting, um, it’s an unusual perspective, but I agree with you.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:19.352
Um, I think there’s also, for me, at least, there’s a kind of stage-of-life thing, as well, around this.

00:01:19.377 --> 00:01:24.144
So, when I was younger, when I was in my teens and my twenties, my friends were really,

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:30.633
really central to what I was doing and how I was feeling and took up all my, um, thinking.

00:01:30.887 --> 00:01:37.587
And as I’ve got older, and had, you know, kids and a family, that’s kind of taken centre stage.

00:01:37.907 --> 00:01:43.256
And while while I still, you know, love my friends they just don’t occupy as central a,

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:45.291
a part of my life as they used to.

00:01:45.711 --> 00:01:51.321
Um, but obviously different people will have different, um, experiences of that as well.

00:01:51.394 --> 00:01:52.842
<v><b>Debbie:</b></v> Yeah, I, I totally agree with you.

00:01:52.875 --> 00:01:57.045
When I was younger, friends were really important, once my children came along,

00:01:57.072 --> 00:01:58.365
that was my main focus.

00:01:58.405 --> 00:02:00.485
Everything that I had was invested in them,

00:02:00.758 --> 00:02:05.119
so therefore my family unit became the most important thing.

00:02:05.433 --> 00:02:08.660
Now I’m older, things have changed in my life.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.005
Recently having gone through a marriage break-up,

00:02:11.758 --> 00:02:15.835
I have now discovered that as much as my family have been there for me,

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:19.897
and my two sons have been my rocks, and they really have been amazing,

00:02:20.563 --> 00:02:24.030
my friends are the ones that have got me through everything,

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:28.663
because they are of the same age as me, the same generation.

00:02:28.869 --> 00:02:34.328
So, they understand what’s going on with me, they understand why I’m feeling the way I’m feeling,

00:02:34.601 --> 00:02:40.410
and so, I feel more comfortable with them and I’m able to respond to any advice

00:02:40.437 --> 00:02:41.523
that they might give me.

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:48.687
Um, so as much as I love my family, right now in my life my friends are more important to me.